e="Calibri"/><a:cs typeface="Calibri"/><a:sym typeface="Calibri"/></a:endParaRPr></a:p><a:p><a:pPr algn="l" indent="-342900" lvl="0" marL="342900" marR="0" rtl="0"><a:lnSpc><a:spcPct val="110000"/></a:lnSpc><a:spcBef><a:spcPts val="540"/></a:spcBef><a:spcAft><a:spcPts val="0"/></a:spcAft><a:buClr><a:schemeClr val="dk1"/></a:buClr><a:buSzPts val="2700"/><a:buFont typeface="Calibri"/><a:buChar char="•"/></a:pPr><a:r><a:rPr b="0" i="0" lang="en-US" sz="2700" u="none"><a:solidFill><a:schemeClr val="dk1"/></a:solidFill><a:latin typeface="Calibri"/><a:ea typeface="Calibri"/><a:cs typeface="Calibri"/><a:sym typeface="Calibri"/></a:rPr><a:t>Most cells contain 20 different aminoacyl-tRNA synthetases, one for each amino acid.</a:t></a:r><a:endParaRPr/></a:p><a:p><a:pPr algn="l" indent="-342900" lvl="0" marL="342900" marR="0" rtl="0"><a:lnSpc><a:spcPct val="110000"/></a:lnSpc><a:spcBef><a:spcPts val="540"/></a:spcBef><a:spcAft><a:spcPts val="0"/></a:spcAft><a:buClr><a:schemeClr val="dk1"/></a:buClr><a:buSzPts val="2700"/><a:buFont typeface="Calibri"/><a:buChar char="•"/></a:pPr><a:r><a:rPr b="0" i="0" lang="en-US" sz="2700" u="none"><a:solidFill><a:schemeClr val="dk1"/></a:solidFill><a:latin typeface="Calibri"/><a:ea typeface="Calibri"/><a:cs typeface="Calibri"/><a:sym typeface="Calibri"/></a:rPr><a:t>Some cells contain fewer than 20 synthetases; in this case, one amino acid is converted to another after charging the tRNA.</a:t></a:r><a:endParaRPr/></a:p></p:txBody></p:sp><p:cxnSp><p:nvCxnSpPr><p:cNvPr id="369" name="Google Shape;369;p52"/><p:cNvCxnSpPr/><p:nvPr/></p:nvCxnSpPr><p:spPr><a:xfrm><a:off x="304800" y="838200"/><a:ext cx="8382000" cy="0"/></a:xfrm><a:prstGeom prst="straightConnector1"><a:avLst/></a:prstGeom><a:noFill/><a:ln cap="flat" cmpd="thinThick" w="57150"><a:solidFill><a:srgbClr val="2FB0DC"/></a:solidFill><a:prstDash val="solid"/><a:miter lim="800000"/><a:headEnd len="med" type="none" w="med"/><a:tailEnd len="med" type="none" w="med"/></a:ln></p:spPr></p:cxnSp><p:pic><p:nvPicPr><p:cNvPr descr="General structure of aminoacyl-tRNAs. 
This has a sideways pentagon where the three right sides are thick. The left point is O, at the top point, adenine bonds right to H. At the top right is H (inside) bonds right to OH and is 2’. At the bottom right is 3’, where H (inside) bonds right to O, which bonds right to C, which double bonds down to O (O, C, and O are all in a pink box). C bonds right to C, which bonds up to H, right to R, and down to +NH3. This is labeled as the aminoacyl group. The bottom point of the pentagon has CH2 bonding right to H and down to O, which bonds down to P, which bonds left to –O, double bonds right to O, and bonds down to O, which sits atop a straight green line traveling downward. This line has a short line joining it at the bottom. This long line is labeled as the amino acid arm and is 5’.  Just above where the second line joins, it is labeled as pG. From here, double lines extend left and right to a circle. The left is labeled as the D arm, and the right circle is labeled as the T[psi]C arm. Below the middle of these is a solid green line to a hollow circle, and is labeled as the anticodon arm. 
